LaTeXbangla 2.0.1 — Writing Bangla & Assamese with ordinary LaTeX

LaTeXbangla switches automatically between your chosen language and English,
preserving your English fonts. It supports bold and italic, native lists and
numbering, references, citations, and text and digits in mathematics.

Getting started

Compile with XeLaTeX or LuaLaTeX. Tested on TeX Live 2026.
For a local installation, copy latexbangla.sty, the latexbangla-*.lbx files and
the complete code/ folder beside your document. Install one of the supported Bangla
fonts, then use its name below:

    \documentclass{article}
    \usepackage{latexbangla}
    \setbanglamainfont{Kalpurush}
    \begin{document}
    \section{বাংলা এবং English}
    বাংলা and English একই অনুচ্ছেদে।
    \textbf{বাংলা and English দুটোই bold।}
    \end{document}

Tested fonts for both languages: Kalpurush, SolaimanLipi, Siyam Rupali,
Noto Serif Bengali, Noto Sans Bengali, Anek Bangla, Tiro Bangla, Galada,
Hind Siliguri, Nikosh, Kohinoor Bangla, Mukti, Baloo Da 2, Lohit Bengali,
Jamrul, Likhan, Shimanto and FreeSerif. Jamrul 0.1 and Likhan 0.6 lack
ৎ (U+09CE). Other Bangla fonts may work too, but have not been tested.
Fonts are not included. If Anek fails to load by name, save its TTF
as AnekBangla-VF.ttf beside your document and use
\setbanglamainfont{AnekBangla-VF.ttf}.

For Assamese documents, use \usepackage[language=assamese]{latexbangla}
and the same font commands. Bangla remains the default language.

Choosing an English font is optional: use \setmainfont as usual if you want
to change it. Automatic numbers use native digits by default; set the package option
changecounternumbering=false to keep the usual numbering. Digits you type
retain their script.

Read latexbangla.pdf for the full guide in English, Bangla and Assamese, with code and
rendered examples, font settings, custom counters and help with older documents.
More examples and bug reports: https://github.com/Neehan/LaTeXbangla

